package owneraccess

const View = `<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Manage while away · Kinosail</title><script src="/static/theme.js?v=electric-1"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/static/app.css?v=electric-1"></head><body class="settings-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><main id="main" class="settings-shell"><a class="back" href="/settings">Settings</a><header class="settings-intro"><h1>Manage while away</h1><p>Your Owner controls stay private. At home, sign in at your local Server address. Away from home, connect a paired device first.</p></header><div class="settings-flow"><section><h2>Private management</h2><p role="status">{{if .Enabled}}{{if eq .State "ready"}}On · waiting for paired devices{{else}}Unavailable · reconnect at home and finish HTTPS setup{{end}}{{else}}Off · manage at home{{end}}</p>{{if .Warning}}<p role="alert">{{.Warning}}</p>{{end}}<p>Watching away from home is a <a href="/settings/remote-readiness">separate setting</a>. Enabling public viewing never enables Owner access.</p>{{if .Enabled}}<form action="/settings/management/disable" method="post"><p>Turning this off disconnects all devices and removes every pairing. You can still manage your Server at home.</p><button>Turn off management while away</button></form>{{else}}<p>Start at home with your Owner account and two-step sign-in. Use your trusted local HTTPS address, or finish <a href="/settings/remote-readiness">public HTTPS setup</a> first.</p><form action="/settings/management/enable" method="post"><label>Public Server hostname and management port<input name="endpoint" value="{{.Endpoint}}" placeholder="your-server.duckdns.org:{{.HostPort}}" maxlength="259" required autocomplete="off" aria-describedby="endpoint-help"></label><p id="endpoint-help">Use your own public hostname, followed by the public UDP port (normally {{.HostPort}}). This is the address your paired device uses to find your Server.</p><button>Enable private management</button></form>{{end}}</section>{{if .Enabled}}<section><h2>Connect your Owner device</h2><ol><li>In your router, forward <strong>UDP {{.PublicPort}}</strong> to <strong>UDP {{.HostPort}}</strong> on this Server. Use <a href="/settings/remote-readiness">the router guides</a> if you need help. Do not forward the Server’s local HTTPS port or put it in a DMZ.</li><li>Install the <a href="https://www.wireguard.com/install/" rel="noreferrer">WireGuard app</a> on your device.</li><li>While at home, name the device below and download its private profile. Import that file into WireGuard on that device, then delete the downloaded copy.</li><li>Turn on the tunnel. Open <a href="{{.URL}}/settings">your private management page</a> and sign in as the same Owner.</li><li>Before leaving home, turn off Wi-Fi and try again using mobile data. Server status cannot confirm that your router or internet provider allows the connection.</li></ol>{{if .AtHome}}<form action="/settings/management/devices" method="post"><label>Device name<input name="label" placeholder="My phone" maxlength="80" required autocomplete="off"></label><p>This device is paired to your current Owner account. Each device needs its own profile.</p><button>Download Owner device profile</button></form>{{else}}<p>Pair new devices when you are home. You can revoke existing devices here.</p>{{end}}<p>Keep the profile private. It opens a connection, but your Owner passkey or password plus authenticator is still required. The tunnel reaches only Kinosail. While it is on, your Server handles this device’s DNS lookups; other internet traffic keeps its normal route.</p></section>{{end}}<section><h2>Paired devices</h2>{{range .Devices}}<form action="/settings/management/devices/revoke" method="post"><p><strong>{{.Label}}</strong></p><button name="publicKey" value="{{.PublicKey}}">Revoke {{.Label}}</button></form>{{else}}<p>No Owner devices are paired.</p>{{end}}</section><section><h2>Lost a device or cannot connect?</h2><p>At home, open your local Server address and sign in as an Owner. Revoke the missing device here, or turn management off to disconnect every device. Sign out lost devices in Settings as well.</p><p>After an Owner’s credentials or security policy change, pair that Owner’s devices again at home. Keep your account recovery codes somewhere safe, away from the paired device.</p><p>For local terminal recovery, run <code>kinosail owner-access-disable</code> inside the Server container. It disconnects private management within two seconds and clears its pairings.</p><p>If your Server is offline or this device was revoked, turn off its WireGuard tunnel to restore normal DNS. If your home internet address changed, turn the tunnel off and on to reconnect.</p><p>If browser secure DNS bypasses the tunnel, use the device’s automatic DNS setting while managing your Server. A certificate warning means you should stop and fix HTTPS setup.</p></section></div></main></body></html>`
